===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|wellen}}, from a merger of {{inh|en|ang|weallan}} (intransitive) and {{m|ang|wiellan}} (transitive), both meaning “to boil.” Further from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wallaną}} and {{m|gem-pro|*wallijaną}}. Doublet of {{m|en|wall}}.

Cognate with {{cog|de|wallen|t=boil, seethe}}, {{cog|da|vælde|t=gush}}, {{cog|nn|vella}} and outside Germanic, with {{cog|sq|valë|t=hot, boiling}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To issue forth, as water from the earth; to flow; to spring.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|10|passage=[Blood] '''welled''' from out the wound.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824|author=w:William Cullen Bryant|title=s:A Forest Hymn
|passage=[Yon spring] '''wells''' softly forth.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have something seep out of the surface.
#: {{ux|en|Her eyes '''welled''' with tears.}}

